---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FollowCta from '../components/FollowCta.astro';

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: Astro.site?.href },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 2,
      name: 'About',
      item: new URL('/about/', Astro.site).href,
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Qivy — practical AI & automation knowledge"
  description="Qivy is a practical knowledge platform for AI, automation and useful technology tips. What it covers, how articles are verified, and how it is built."
  schemas={[breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="hdr wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]} />
    <h1>About Qivy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Qivy is a practical knowledge platform for AI, automation and everyday
      technology. Not a news portal — a place to find one useful thing and
      apply it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What Qivy is for</h2>
      <p>
        There is no shortage of writing about what AI might do. There is much
        less about what you can do with it this afternoon. Qivy exists for the
        second kind: short, specific, practical pieces that change something
        in how you work.
      </p>
      <p>The test every article has to pass is simple — <strong>can you do
      something differently in the next ten minutes?</strong> If not, it
      doesn't get published.</p>

      <h2>What it covers</h2>
      <p>Four areas, weighted deliberately unequally:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>AI</strong> — tools and applications, assistants like
          ChatGPT and Claude, coding agents, local and open-source models,
          prompting, and the features people pay for but never use.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Automation</strong> — n8n workflows, Python scripts, Google
          Sheets, API integrations, webhooks and self-hosted setups that take
          repetitive work off your hands.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tips &amp; Tricks</strong> — Windows, browsers, editors and
          the everyday software you already have open.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tools</strong> — a reference list rather than an article
          stream, with the use case and the date each price was checked.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pricing and availability are written India-first where that changes the
        answer, and left global where it doesn't — a prompting technique isn't
        regional, and pretending otherwise just pads the article.
      </p>

      <h2>How articles are verified</h2>
      <p>
        Every article carries one of two badges, and the difference is real:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Tested</strong> — someone actually ran this. The steps come
          from doing it, not reading about it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Researched</strong> — verified against official
          documentation, but not personally run. The sources are listed at the
          bottom of the article so you can check them yourself.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That distinction matters because part of Qivy's publishing is assisted
        by automation, and being straight about it is the whole point. A
        drafted article is never marked <em>Tested</em> — only a human who
        actually ran the steps can set that. Nothing is published without a
        person reading it first.
      </p>
      <p>What Qivy will not do: invent benchmarks, invent pricing, invent
      personal experience, rewrite someone else's article, or publish old
      information as though it were current.</p>

      <h2>The Maker archive</h2>
      <p>
        Qivy started out writing about 3D printing — printer reviews, slicer
        settings, and a filament knowledge base covering PLA, PETG, TPU, ABS
        and Nylon. That material is still here, in the
        <a href="/maker/">Maker archive</a>, because it is still useful and
        people still find it. It simply isn't what the site leads with any
        more.
      </p>

      <h2>How this site is built</h2>
      <p>
        Deliberately boring, because boring keeps working. Qivy is a static
        site: articles are Markdown files, built by
        <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a>
        into plain HTML and served from GitHub Pages. Almost no JavaScript
        reaches your browser — code highlighting happens at build time, and the
        only interactive pieces are the theme toggle, the menu and the
        copy-code buttons.
      </p>
      <p>
        Publishing works through pull requests: a draft arrives as a proposed
        change, gets reviewed and edited, and goes live when it is merged.
        That gives every article a review step and a permanent history,
        without a content management system to maintain.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Found an error, or something that no longer works the way an article
        says? That's worth telling me about — corrections are the most useful
        feedback a site like this gets.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/fshbet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          github.com/fshbet
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <FollowCta compact />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hdr {
    padding-block: var(--sp-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bd);
  }
  h1 {
    margin-top: var(--sp-5);
    font-size: var(--fs-3xl);
  }
  .lede {
    margin-top: var(--sp-4);
  }
  .body {
    padding-top: var(--sp-7);
  }
</style>
